<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sho Shisho</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1421</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Shō Shishō</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sho Shisho was king of Chuzan on Okinawa from 1407 to 1421 and the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sho-dynasty">First Sho dynasty</a>. In 1406 he and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-hashi">Sho Hashi</a> overthrew King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bunei">Bunei</a> and moved the capital to Shuri. He received recognition from Ming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> as king, while his son led the campaigns that soon united Okinawa as the Ryukyu Kingdom.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The Ryukyuan official histories describe Shishō as the son of Lord Samekawa of Iheya, a small island near Okinawa; the Chūzan Seifu, citing a collection of legends, the Irosetsuden, adds that he had one sister, later the priestess of Baten near Sashiki. Some scholars trace the family to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>. The novelist Takizawa Bakin linked it to the port of Sashiki in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/higo-province">Higo Province</a>, and in the 1930s Orikuchi Shinobu read a mention of Yashiro in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omoro-soshi">Omoro Sōshi</a> as the city of Yatsushiro.</p><p>Following Orikuchi, the historian Gregory Smits traces the family to the Nawa clan of Yatsushiro and suggests they were seafarers of the Southern Court, possibly wokou <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, who migrated to Okinawa. In 1392 Shishō and Hashi were said to have founded Sashiki Castle, with Hashi as its lord.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Later histories portray King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bunei">Bunei</a> as cruel and decadent; in 1406 he had men castrated and sent as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a>, who ordered them returned. The official histories say Hashi then led the rebellion that took Shuri Castle and set his father on the throne. Shishō's delegation to the Ming court in 1407 claimed that he was Bunei's son, while another claimant to be Bunei's heir had already arrived in late 1405. The Chinese merchant-official Ō Mō served as chief minister for parts of the reigns of Shishō and Hashi, and Chinese influence grew markedly, possibly a sign of Chinese support for the coup.</p><p>Little else is recorded of him. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chuzan-seikan">Chūzan Seikan</a> mentions him only as Hashi's father, and the 1701 edition of the Chūzan Seifu gives him a brief paragraph that mainly lists his envoys. Smits calls him a &quot;name or banner under which Okinawan entities conducted tribute trade&quot;.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Shishō died in 1421 and was buried at the tomb of Sashiki yōdore, cut into a cliff beside Sashiki Castle and moved to another cliff in 1764 after damage. According to the Chūzan Seifu, the Xuande Emperor granted Hashi the surname Shō in 1430, which was then applied to his father, though Chinese records do not mention it. Shishō also received the divine name Kimishimamono, and many later Okinawan families claimed descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sho-dynasty">First Shō dynasty</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shishō” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sho Hashi, the lord of Sashiki in the south, overthrew King Bunei of Chuzan in 1406 and installed his father, <b>Sho Shisho</b>, as king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1406 <b>Sho Shisho</b> and his son Sho Hashi overthrew King Bunei and moved the capital to Shuri.</blockquote><a href="/state/chuzan">Chuzan</a></li></ul></section>
